  "worked_solution": "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(1)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2(2)}=\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{4}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}={(-0.4)}^{2}=0.16.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2n} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(-0.4)}^{2}}{1-0.16} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}(0.16)}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.4}{0.84} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
